  "source_path": "/scratch/ziv_baretto/Thesis_Ziv/Capstone-Thesis-/DATA_SET_BUILDER_AND_EXPLORER/Timeline_Maker/output_merged_v3/food_safety/State Of H.P vs Gopal Sharma on 28 October, 2015.json",
  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "State Of H.P vs Gopal Sharma on 28 October, 2015 Author: Rajiv Sharma Bench: Rajiv Sharma In The High Court Of Himachal Pradesh, Shimla. Cr. Appeal No. 153 of 2006. Reserved on: October 27, 2015 Decided on: October 2 . State of H.P. .... Versus Gopal Sharma ... Coram The Hon'ble Mr. Justice Rajiv Sharma, Judge. Whether approved for reporting? 1 of No.",
    "facts": "The case of the prosecution, in a nut shell, is that on 30.9.2004, Food Inspector, Shimla visited business premises of the accused known as 'Sharma Dhaba' at Taradevi and found him conducting State Of H.P vs Gopal Sharma on 28 October, 2015 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/88796114/ 1 business. Eatables, biscuits, fruit drinks, milk etc. were kept for sale to the public. On demand, he failed to produce licence for sale of food articles. The sample of the food was also taken for analysis from the shop. The proceedings were launched against the accused. 3. The complainant examined three witnesses. The statement of the accused under Section 313 Cr.P.C. was also recorded. The accused examined two witnesses in defence. The accused was convicted and sentenced by the learned Magistrate after completion of the trial on of 27.7.2005. The accused preferred an appeal against the judgment dated 27.7.2005 before the learned Sessions Judge, Shimla. The learned rt Sessions Judge, Shimla, allowed the same on 4.3.2006. Hence, this appeal at the instance of the State. 4. PW-1 Leela Dhar, Food Inspector deposed that he considered the accused to be proprietor of the Shop on his admission. However, he was unable to say if Vineet Kumar Proprietor had applied for renewal of the licence which was granted in his favour. He denied that accused had claimed himself to be servant in the shop. PW-2 Ashok Kumar, Peon also stated that accused was owner of the shop. He was unable to say whether the shop was that of Vineet Kumar and on that day Vineet Kumar had obtained licence or not. 5. DW-1 Prem Kumar testified that he has building at Taradevi. The shop was rented out to Vineet Kumar. Vineet Kumar was running business in the shop. Accused never remained his tenant. DW-2 Jeet Ram, Supervisor of the Health Department, on the basis of official record, stated that Vineet Sharma applied for a licence on 30.9.2004 and on the same day he had deposited the licence fee in the office. Licence No. 231/04 was issued in favour of Vineet Sharma. It was prepared on . 30.9.2004. In the licence the address was given of Vineet Sharma, Taradevi. The licence would be deemed to be valid on and after 30.9.2004. He also stated in his cross-examination that earlier Vineet Kumar had obtained licence for the year 2003 and 2004 on 1.9.2003 which expired on 31.3.2004. He got the licence renewed only on of 30.9.2004. 6. It has come on record that 'Sharma Dhaba' was owned and rt operated by Vineet Kumar. He has obtained the licence w.e.f. 30.9.2004. The Food Inspector had visited the shop in the absence of Vineet Kumar. It is, in these circumstances, PW-1 Leela Dhar, Food Inspector, considered the accused to be the proprietor of the shop on his admission. Thus, the learned Sessions Judge, Shimla, has correctly come to the conclusion that it was not the accused who was running the shop/Dhaba without possessing the licence under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act and Rules.",
